15:51:55 out   are no function entry addresses and no pseudo-C for this original**. The exact
15:51:55 out   tool results were:
15:51:55 out   | Probe | Result |
15:51:55 out   |---|---|
15:51:55 out   | `core_target_open` | kind `unknown`, description “A file we don't recognise yet”, `raw: null` |
15:51:55 out   | `core_target_triage` | kind `unknown`, description “A file we don't recognise yet”, recommended toolset `core` only |
15:51:55 out   | `native_functions_list` | `not_supported`: “Couldn't read this as a program: the file is malformed (Unknown file magic). … complete ELF, PE, Mach-O or DOS program” |
15:51:55 out   | `native_strings_find` (`crop`) | `not_supported`: same “Unknown file magic” refusal |
15:51:55 out   | `native_strings_find` (`_karp`) | `not_supported`: same “Unknown file magic” refusal |
15:51:55 out   | `native_function_pseudocode` (`main`) | `not_supported`: same “Unknown file magic” refusal |
15:51:55 out   Because the interpreter, not a binary, is the original, the equivalent of
15:51:55 out   “find the string, follow the reference, read the function” was done on the
15:51:55 out   source itself: every string below was found in `ultracrop.py`, followed to the
15:51:55 out   method that emits it, and the method body was read in full. Section 15 is that
15:51:55 out   ledger: each rule → the exact Python function → its line span → its byte
15:51:55 out   offset in the file.
15:51:55 out   So “addresses” below are **source locations**: `ultracrop.py:<function> L<first>-<last>`.
15:51:55 out   They identify the exact code that defines each rule, and the formulas were
15:51:55 out   copied from those bodies. No decompiler output was used or fabricated.
15:51:55 out   ---
